C Language

    Data types - 
        Primitive datatypes are the types that are not objects and stores all sorts of data.
        Keywords to modify the basic integer datatype: short, long, and unsigned

    Enums - 
        A datatype that allows a programmer to define a variable and specify the valid value that could be stored into that variable
        eg. enum primaryColour {red, yellow, blue};
        enum primaryColour myColor, gregsColor;
        The compiler treats enumeration identifiers as integer constants

    Format Specifiers - 
        they specify the type of data of the variable to be displayed

    Command line arguments -
        When main() is called by the runtime system, two arguments are actually passed to the function:
        -the first argument(argc for argument count) is an integer value that specifies the number of arguments passed to the function
        -the second argument(argv for argument vector) is an array of character pointers(strings)

    int main(int argc, char *argv[])
    {
    ...
    }

    Bitwise operators

        - bitwise operator and shift operators
        - operates in bits in integer values

    Cast operator: the type cast operator has a higher precedence than all the arithmetic operators except the unary minus and unary plus operator
        (int)21.51+(int)26.99 -> 21+26

    sizeof operator:
        evaluated at compile time and not runtime, unless a variable-length array is used in its argument

    Operator precedence: highest to lowest as we go to the bottom-

        Postfix: () [] -> . ++ -- : Left to right
        Unary: + - ! ~ ++ -- (type) * & sizeof : Right to Left 
        Multiplicative: * / % : Left to right
        Additive: + - : Left to right
        Shift: << >> : Left to right
        Relational: < <= > >= :Left to right
        Equality: == != : Left to right
        Bitwise AND: & : Left to right
        Bitwise XOR: ^ : Left to right
        Bitwise OR: | : Left to right
        Logical AND: && : Left to right
        Logical OR: || : Left to right
        Conditional: ?: : Right to Left
        Assignment: = += -= *= /= %= >>= <<= &= ^= |= : Right to Left
        Comma: , : Left to right

    2D arrays:
		int numbers[3][4] = 
			{
				{..........}, //first row
				{..........}, //second row
				{..........}  //third row
			};
		
		It is not required to initialize all elements in a 2D array
		Rest of the elements are initialized to 0.
		
		Designated initializers:
			int numbers[3][4] ={[0][0]=1, [1][1]=5, [2][2]=9}; //rest elements are initialized to 0
	
	Variable length arrays:
		The term variable length array does not mean that you can modify the length of the array after you create it
		A VLA keeps same size after creation
		A VLA allows you to specify the size of an array with a variable when creating an array.
		You cannot initialize a VLA in its declaration.
			float a8[n];
			float a9[m];
	
	Functions:
		A function is a self-contained unit of program code designed to accomplish a particular task.
		[imp] When passing an array as an argument to a function, you must also pass an additional argument specifying the size of the array. 
		The function has no means of knowng how many elements are there in the array.
		When invokinga function, the arguments you specify when you call a function should agree in type, number, and sequence with which the paramters are passed in the function header.		
		
	Strings:
		Single char is assigned between '<>'
		String constant or string literal is a sequence of characters or symbols b/w pair of double-quote characters "<>"
		A string is always terminated by a null char, so the length of string is always one greater than the number of charachters in the string.
		We cannot assign strings directly to charachter arrays in c-lang, however, we can declare individual chars in the declared charachterarray with single char literals.
			char s[100];
			s="hello"; //doesnt work
			
			s[0]='h'; //this works

	Character String:
		char myString[20];  //in this we can store a string of only 19 length, last one is for the null char "\0"
		char word[] = {'H','E','L','L','O','!'};
		
	Giving string as input:
		char input[10];
		scanf("%s",input);  //we dont need the & operator to use on string while using scanf
		
	Constant strings:
		#define BEEP '\a'
		#define TEE 'T'
		#define ESC '\033'
		
		C has a way yo create symbolic constants using 'enums'
		
		const char message[]="<string>";
	
	Common string functions:
	    Need to used <string.h> library
		strlen - length of a string
			returned as size_t
		strcpy() & strncpy()- copying one char string to another
			strcpy does not check whether the source string fits in the target string or not, so strncpy is the safer choice which checks
		strcat() & strncat()- concatenation of 2 strings
		strcmp() & strncmp()- comparing two strings
